In article <id.5MJB1.TYJ@nmti.com>, rwp@nmti.com (roland pringle) writes:

|>I get no link errors.  Only upon execution of the Fortran program do I get an
|>error that says it is unable to map libxxx.so.  Libxxx.so will vary depending
|>upon the order of the shared libraries entered on the link command, i.e.,
|>f77 -called_shared xthreads main.o -L/path/to -lxxx -lyyy -lzzz
|>

Here's a couple of tests you could try:

	1) DBX the executable and type the command

		listobj

	   That will tell you where the executable is looking for libraries.

	2) Set the environmental variable _RLD_ARGS to "-trace" or "-v".
	   That will make the loader trace it's actions.  Try "-v" first:
	   it produces less output.

	3) Is your program running setuid or setgid?  The loader then
	   restricts the library search path to the default directories
	   (/usr/shlib /usr/ccs/lib /usr/cmplrs/cc /usr/lib /usr/local/lib).
	
	4) Have you tried setting the library's SONAME when you build it?
	   Use the -soname option to do this:

		ld -shared -soname /path/to/libxxx.so -o libxxx.so *.o

	   This will force the loader to load "/path/to/libxxx.so" at
	   runtime.

						Frank


