In article <gurman-2707941132230001@barkochba.gsfc.nasa.gov>, gurman@uvsp.gsfc.nasa.gov (Joseph B. Gurman) writes...
>    Hardware: HP 4SiMX with first-generation JetDirect interfaces (i.e.,
>no lpd support, only telnet stream)
>    Hosts supported: a few Macs, several OpenVMS VAX or AXP machines, and
>a slew of OSF/1 AXP workstations
> 
[stuff deleted]
>    Problem: when printing from anything other than the Macs, when the
>printer is left in auto-emulation-recognize mode, the last page of the
>print job is delayed for some 10's of seconds of timeout(?) before
>printing, and some PostScript jobs (apparently those lacking the explicit
>EOF character the printer expects from PC hosts) never print, OR if the
>queue is configured with an explicit EOF at the end of every job, the
>printer ejects a blank page between every job, even if successive jobs are
>the same type (i.e. PostScript right after PostScript or ASCII [PCL] after
>ASCII).

We currently use Milan MIL-3100 print servers not the HP network cards,
but the network connection shouldn't matter.

Use <ESC>E<ESC>%-12345X at the end of each print job.  The <ESC>E is a
HPPCL reset command which will flush the printer's buffer.  If the
buffer contains any information, the page will be ejected.  This will
work for all LaserJet models.  The <ESC>%-12345X is new HPPJL reset
command which only works for the LaserJet 4 series.  I always use
<ESC>E and haven't found the second reset sequence necessary.

I've got to thank the people at Goddard who worked on the SEWP contract.
It has saved us a lot of grief in procuring printers.

