>Has anybody ported xlock to OSF1 V2.0 with ENHANCED SECURITY ?
>
>Are there arguments against running xlock with root permissions,
>e.g. known security holes in the code ?
>
I have been thinking about the same issues.  I would be very hesitant
to run xlock setuid to root.  Since it wasn't designed to run that way,
you might well be opening up a nasty can of worms.  But since only 
a process running with root priviledge can get at the encrypted passwords
under enhanced security, we have a problem.  I was thinking of writing
a simple user authentication daemon which would be passed a user name
and a clear-text password, and it would tell you whether it was right
or wrong.  It would deliberately have a long delay in it and do 
ridiculous amounts of logging to prevent its use as a password-guessing
routine.  It might also be possible to make it accept requests only
from processes running with a certain gid, and then make programs that
need to issue requests to it setgid to that, thus reducing your risks
some.  An better alternative is to make something which works like NIS+
under Solaris 2.3.  That is, a user can see his or her own encrypted
password, but no one elses.  Dunno, can anyone think of a better way 
around this?

