This is an AVS thing. I use AVS on both Sun and Alpha AXP and they both
have this problem. I have confered with AVS about it, and they explain that
this is what happens anytime you exit AVS not through the main menu.
I suppose that AVS has a destruct function that cleans these all out, otherwise
there is nothing to delete them when AVS processes die. 
It would be nice if there were a utility (aside from reboot) that would clean
out shmsegs that are not attached, but, not knowing of any, I use ipcrm -m
for all the segs. (Quite a bother). One thing that I wonder about is why
is /usr/bin/X11/X creating these shmsegs too? These appear to just pile up
and are perhaps just fragmenting the available memory? On SunOS, there does
not appear to be anything systemwise that creates these, why so on the DEC?
