Thanks for the info.  I did a little playing around and found that under
some circumstances of exiting AVS "abnormally", segs are deleted.  Anyway,
here is a little procedure I worked out on our Alpha to remove segments
that have no processes attached.  It's not guaranteed for all systems, it's
not elegant, and it doesn't cleanup all the segments that I would like it
to, but it's a start...

  ipcs -mo | tail +4 | awk '{if ($7 == 0) print $2}' | xargs -t -i ipcrm -m
{}

(There are several segments on our system now that show processes attached
to them, but no users or active PIDs associated with them, so a neat
enhancement would be to match-up active PIDs with the PIDs from the ipcs
command and process accordingly.  Any takers?)  I guess it could be run by
cron periodically.  We don't re-boot too often, so this procedure should
help.  At one point we had about 30-40 orphaned segments and were running
out of shared memory even though half of our 256Mbytes of main memory are
setup for shared memory, so it's a real problem!
Peyton

