In article <potterCtK1o8.98F@netcom.com>,
David Potter <potter@netcom.com> wrote:
>This is quite normal for shared memory.  Even programs that 
>end normally leave their shared memory around, unless they explicitly
>remove it.

More than normal; it is required behavior. A program can detach, then
re-attach, and expect to find data still there.

I don't think that segments that are not currently mapped into a process
are actually locked into physical memory, even if they aren't deleted.
As far as I can tell, they only take up swap space (is there a separate
swap area for shared memory segments?).

Also, it looks to me as if you can delete a segment even while it is
in use, and not cause any problems for those processes that already have
it attached. In fact, I don't see any real difference between using
shared memory segments and doing mmap()/mlock() (except that mlock()
is privileged, and you can keep segments persistent over a reboot, and
you have more flexibility as to naming a segment - the whole UNIX file
system name space).


