In article <CtK31o.Bnr@serval.net.wsu.edu>, boyerj@coopext.cahe.wsu.edu (Jim Boyer) writes:

>Hi, I am new to UNIX system admin and would like some advice on DEC's
>OSF/1 file system.  The question is whether the AdvFS is worth converting
>to on a DEC Alpha machine, or should I stay with the standard UFS?  One
>source whom I respect says that I should stay with UFS.  Anybody have
>an opinion on this subject?

For some I/O loads and many file system management problems the
Advanced File System offers some advantages over UFS.  For example
if you have an unstable system, not needing to spend minutes or
tens of minutes having fsck(8) check the file systems can be a
big win.  On file system that have lots of file transations, the
logging offered by the Advanced File System may offer better
performance than the synchronous update that UFS uses.

Probably the biggest advantage comes from space management.  If
a UFS file systems is running out of space you either need to
migrate the whole thing to larger partition.  This will probably
taking it out of service to do the backup/restore.  Even if you
only move a large directory tree to its own partition, that is
going to require careful on the part of the system manager to
make sure nothing is lost.

With the Advanced File System if a file set or domain are running
out of space you just another disk or partition to the domain.
With the layered product utilities you can get other services.
I think Striping, deleted file recovery and file set cloning 
are features that come with the utilities.

