References: <Jul.21.13.26.56.1994.5252@pilot.njin.net> <1994Jul25.194141.5530@datum.nyo.dec.com> <CtJn9J.75u@helios.physics.utoronto.ca> <CtK4Iz.Esq@helios.physics.utoronto.ca>
Date: Tue, 26 Jul 1994 19:25:45 GMT
Lines: 77

Earlier today, I wrote ...

| I just got an e-mail message from Trin Tantsetthi <trin@nucleus.nectec.or.th>,
| who cleared up the confusion.  It turns out that "-terminate" is documented in
| the "Xdec" man page, whereas I'd been looking in the "Xserver" man page (which
| is, after all, where Frank claimed I'd find it).  But to add to the ambiguity,
| one observes that "man Xserver" produces a man page which starts out ...
|
| 	XSERVER(1)
|
| 	NAME
| 	  Xserver - X Window System server
|
| ... whereas "man Xdec" produces one which start out ...
|
| 	Xdec(1X)
|
| 	NAME
| 	  Xdec, Xserver - X Window System server
|
| ... so one could argue that the "Xdec" man page *is* the (well, is an) Xserver
| man page; it's just not the one you get when you type "man Xserver".

... and I thought that was the end of this thread.  Until I got a message from
Richard Eckman <eckman@eos1.larc.nasa.gov>, saying:

|   Just to add to the confusion, I do get the Xdec page when I type
| "man Xserver".  The contents of /usr/man/man1/Xserver.1X are:
|
| .so man1/Xdec.1X
|
|   So, it's more or less a symbolic link to Xdec.1X.  Sounds like
| you've somehow installed a different Xserver man page compared
| with the one that comes standard with OSF/1 v2.0

So I checked, and in fact *our* Xserver.1X contains the same thing as his does!
Then I realized that "man Xserver" was actually giving me the MIT X11R5 Xserver
man page (which we have in /usr/local/man, since we have vanilla MIT X11R5 for
our SunOS users, who want an alternative to the loathsome OpenWindows).

At first I thought I must have a MANPATH defined, which had /usr/local/man in
front of /usr/share/man.  But, no, no MANPATH was defined.  After some fussing
around, I verified that

	man -M /usr/share/man Xserver	==> DEC's Xserver man page
and
	man -M /usr/local/man Xserver	==> MIT's Xserver man page
but
	man Xserver			==> MIT's, not DEC's

Why?  Well, it turns out that the files are named

	/usr/share/man/man1/Xserver.1X
and
	/usr/local/man/man1/Xserver.1

Despite the fact that /usr/share/man comes before /usr/local/man in the default
MANPATH, the "man" command seems to search all the directories for "Xserver.1",
before rescanning them again to look for "Xserver.1X".  This was easy to check:
If I rename /usr/share/man/man1/Xserver.1X to /usr/share/man/man1/Xserver.1 (or,
alternatively, /usr/local/man/man1/Xserver.1 to /usr/local/man/man1/Xserver.1X),
then "man Xserver" will give DEC's Xserver man page.  As long as both man pages
have the same name, all is well.

Also, if there's an "Xserver.1" in /usr/local/man/cat1, "man Xserver" will pick
that one in preference to /usr/share/man/man1/Xserver.1X, though it will choose
/usr/share/man/man1/Xserver.1 over /usr/local/man/cat1/Xserver.1, curiously.

So, my question is:  Is this behavious intentional, or is it a bug?  And if the
former, what's the rationale?

