In article <Jul.21.13.26.56.1994.5252@pilot.njin.net>, shevett@pilot.njin.net (Shayde) writes:

|> We keep running out of memory.  It looks like the X server on the console
|> has a hole in it, and I found it yesterday with a size of 26 meg. [...]  
|> The only way I've been able to get things cleared up is a reboot.
|> 

Try editing the file /usr/lib/X11/xdm/Xservers and adding the "-terminate"
option to the X server invocation.

	:0 local /usr/bin/X11/X -terminate

That will cause the server to exit on logout.  Xdm will start a new
instance, and memory will be reclaimed.

Another possibility is to edit /usr/lib/X11/Xserver.conf and remove
the lines that load extensions you don't normally use.

To avoid grief, remember to save a copy of each file before you go
making changes.


|> I'm also getting process table full errors.  ps aux report s 273 processes
|> running, which ain't all that much.

Try changing the "maxusers" parameter in the system configuration file,
/usr/sys/conf/SYSTEMNAME.  Make the parameter larger.  Then run
doconfig -c SYSTEMNAME and rebuild the kernel.

I would also check the to see if there were daemons that don't need to
run.  The SNMP daemons are good candidates if you don't use them.  To
avoid running them, rename the link /sbin/rc3.d/S50common_agent to some
name that does not begin with an "S", like disabled_S50common_agent.

Your original posting indicated that you were using a 96 Meg system with
128 Meg of paging area.  If you can find a second paging partition or
enlarge the current one, you might reduce some of the effects of "bottoming
out."

Hope this helps.

					Frank


