In article <1994Jul18.180219.17019@decvax.dec.com> you write:
:
: In article <301912$5te@masala.cc.uh.edu>, tony@uh.edu (Tony Alvarez)
writes:
: |>
: |>I have been trying to use the following system clock calls on OSF/1
ver 2.0:
: |>
: |>
: |>times(), gettimer(), getclock(), clock_gettime()
:
: Have you tried the man pages? They all have entries (the last in the
: realtime man pages).
:
: times: return process execution times. It seems to be working.
: 
The manual entry for times is brain dead. 
time_t times() is wrong!! It returns clock_t (see the header file
<sys/times.h>). 

Also the RETURN VALUES of the manual page reports that it returns
"time in clock ticks since an arbitrary reference time"

This is a POSIX standard so can be used for portable software. But is is
not the best one to use for new software, I guess that clock_gettime()
is "better" as it is in Draft POSIX and has finer granularity without
the  
danger of overflow which is inherent in times().

TTFN Philip  

PS to convert that to somthing usefull sysconf(_SC_CLK_TCK) will return
the number of tick per second so to calculate time to 10th of a second

#define CT sysconf(_SC_CLK_TCK)
#define TENTHS 10
#define SUBSEC TENTHS
#define _getTimeout(t) (((long)t * (long)SUBSEC ) / (long)CT )



