In article <30416i$5lo@rebold.regent.e-technik.tu-muenchen.de>, pak@regent.e-technik.tu-muenchen.de (Peter A Krauss) writes:
> 
> How do I obtain a better real time clock resolution
> than 976 usec on a Alpha?
> 

The highest resolution you get by using the process cycle counter (PCC),
which runs with a fraction from N=1..16 of the CPU clock. For the
21064 N is 1. For more information see pg. (I) 4-118 of the Alpha
Architecture Reference Manual. Here is a little example, I use to time
short code sequences (using the extended asm features of GCC):

  register unsigned long start, stop;
  unsigned time;

    asm ("rpcc %0" : "=r" (start));
    /* insert the instructions you want to time here */
    asm ("rpcc %0" : "=r" (stop));

    time= (stop&0xffffffff)-(start&0xffffffff);


-- 
May your disk buffers flush before powerdown!
Sebastian


