I have noticed the following problem when using shared libraries on OSF/1 2.0.
I have a program which loads shared libraries dynamically using dlopen/dlsym.
This program is compiled with only the C library.

Now if I create shared libraries that use libc.so, libm.so, or even libcxx.so
everything is fine when loaded with dlopen.  However if the library 
has dependencies of the form -lpthreads -lmach -lcxx -lc_r -lc, and the
library contains global c++ constructors which make pthreads calls then
the library cannot be properly loaded and initialized.  The library itself
is loaded ok it seems, but when the c++ constructors are called they attempt
to call the pthreads routines and they never return.  They just chew up
lots of cpu spinning around in various cma routines like cma__alloc_mem.

This problem goes away if the program which calls dlopen is linked with
-lpthreads -lmach -lcxx -lc_r, but this seems ridiculous and unecessary.
I believe the problem may be caused by empty dependency lists in
libtphreads.so and libmach.so.  odump -D shows they have no dependencies
which cannot be correct.

Does anyone know the true cause of this problem?

Is DEC going to fix it?

George Scott
Johns Hopkins University / Applied Physics Laboratory
gscott@aurora.jhuapl.edu

