    exit( 1 );
  }

  syslog( LOG_NOTICE, "second" );
  fprintf( stderr, "second\n" );
  fflush( stderr );

  exit( 0 );
}
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

when I run it as root it displays both fprintfs but only the first
syslog messages comes through.


- What I think is happening?
The syslogd daemon uses UNIX-domain socket /dev/log to get the
messages from programs. Now that socket is opened BEFORE the chroot()
call and I think that the chroot() CLOSES that descriptor because it
references outside the new file-tree. This is not the case in
SunOS4.x. The chroot() manual page doesn't mention anything about this.

- Question:
So my question is Does the OSF1/2.0 chroot() work correctly or is this
a bug? (Someone should try out this test in their host).

- And finally the solution:
I have a workaround for this problem: I have a syslog-caster which
listens a UNIX-domain socket (/tmp/dev/log with the test program)
and resends all the syslog messages to the real syslog.

