I can suggest three solutions:
a) Don't bother.  Unless you need to create files bigger than the space
  available on a single disk, or have the possibility of unpredictable
  growth in some directory, you can probably get by just fine by having
  rz5 mounted as /home and rz6 mounted as /usr/local, for example.
  But that's not what you want to do, so...
b) Use AdvFS, with the extra-cost utilities.  This is the most flexible
  solution, since you can subdivide the space if you want to, and you
  get other nifties like striping, clone filesets, trashcans, etc.
c) Use the Logical Volume Manager, which also comes free with OSF/1.
  With LVM you can strap several disks together and make them
  look like a single disk, on which you then build a Unix FileSystem.

One other point, by the way.  By tying disks together using method (b) or (c)
you are making failure a more difficult proposition.  If any one disk
in the set fails, the whole set is unavailable.
