>
>Instead of "/disk5" and "/disk6", I want to combine these two drives (and
>more in the future) so they look like one contiguous directory structure;

A summary of the choices, some of which have been mentioned by
others:

1.  The Advanced File System.  Probably the most flexible solution
    since it allows creating a file system that can easily be extended
    as new disks are added.

2.  The Logical Volume Manager.  The only "array" solution supported
    by LVM is volume concatenation.  While LVM does support the dynamic
    addition of more space to a volume, the file system you put on top
    of it may not.  UFS doesn't and I don't know how well LVM and Advfs
    get along.  An important limitation of using LVM is that it is being
    retired in favor of the Logical Storage Manager (LSM), which provides
    more features.

3.  LSM, the replacement for LVM.  In addition to volume concatentation
    it offers the ability to create mirrored and striped volumes.  In
    V2.0 it is offered as a layered product, but will probably included
    with the base system in future versions.  I'd guess that the features
    it offers beyond LVM will be avaialble under seperate license, but
    that's just a guess.

