To my surprise, OSF/1 2.0 does not have a remote version of vdump and vrestore.
The best solution I could come up with was to use use a remote shell running
a dd, eg.

# vdump -0uf - | rsh sam dd of=/dev/vt0 bs=60k

There has been no problems backing up a file system using this method.
However, I have not been able to find a suitable way of interactively
restoring files using vrestore.

First I tried

# rsh sam dd if=/dev/vt0 bs=60k | vrestore -ivf -

vrestore does not open /dev/tty for its interactive input as restore does.
After a bit of thinking, I decided to try a named pipe :-

# mknod /tmp/PIPE p
# rsh -n sam dd if=/dev/vt0 bs=60k > /tmp/PIPE &
# vrestore -ivf /tmp/PIPE

This does not work.  If the file type is a pipe, vrestore will *NOT* use the
specified file, and use *Standard Input* instead.
I verified this by running trace on the command :-

