Wayne Knowles <wdk@rda.frc.maf.govt.nz> wrote:
># mknod /tmp/PIPE p
># rsh -n sam dd if=/dev/vt0 bs=60k > /tmp/PIPE &
># vrestore -ivf /tmp/PIPE
>
>This does not work.  If the file type is a pipe, vrestore will *NOT* use the
>specified file, and use *Standard Input* instead.
>I verified this by running trace on the command :-

Have you checked that 'mknod filename p' actually *made* a pipe?
Unless you have got (and applied) patch OSFV20-053 'mknod filename p'
creates a character file instead of a pipe!

This would certainly explain why vrestore doesn't use the 'pipe'

In addition to this there is a note in the Release Notes:

The vdump and vrestore commands does not support the use of named
pipes for the input/output devices as an argument to the -f flag


