GNU finger is really old and hasn't been updated in eons.  I think the
ICSI hack of GNU finger is substantially newer.  You can try that instead:

ftp.icsi.berkeley.edu:/pub/stolcke/icsi-finger*

>So I figure I'd get gdb since we don't have a license to debug anything but
>the kernel (which is pretty lame, but hey, it's the way it is).

Yeah, everybody hates crippleware... but who's listening?

>And I find that gdb doesn't support the alpha CPU, and/or OSF/1.

Did you try "alpha-dec-osf1" as your configure argument?  gdb 4.12 seems
to grok that string as a valid configuration.  When I look in the 
~gdb-4.12/configure.in file I see:

case "${target}" in
  alpha-dec-osf1*)
    noconfigdirs="$noconfigdirs emacs fileutils grep gas libg++ libio newlib"
    ;;
  alpha*-*-*)
    noconfigdirs="$noconfigdirs gas libg++ libio newlib"
    ;;
...etc...

And when I configure it that way, I see the following CFLAGS worked out which
sure look like there's some understanding of the Alpha:

grep HOST_64_BIT sysdep.h > 64
sed -e 's/@WORDSIZE@/64/' -e '/64-bit.*sysdep.h/ r 64' < ./bfd-in2.h > bfd.h-new
./../move-if-change bfd.h-new bfd.h
rm -f 64
touch stmp-bfd.h
gcc -c -g -DHOST_64_BIT="long " -DOSF_CORE  -I. -I. -I./../include  archive.c
...etc...

I haven't used it yet (just started compiling) but that seems to indicate
at least some work on an Alpha port.  By your post it didn't sound like you
got that far.  If it doesn't work, you might try asking on gnu.gdb.bug
with regard to the state of the Alpha/OSF port of GDB.

