In article 7L@biles.com, daniels@biles.com (Brad Daniels) writes:
>I got a bunch of new PAKs for my DEC OSF system last month, but I couldn't
>register them because I already had active PAKS.  Now some of the previously
>active PAKs have expired, and others haven't.  I want to register all the new
>PAKs so I don't have to keep going into lmfsetup every few days as the old
>ones expire.  Is there a way to do what I want?  lmfsetup won't let me.
>
>- Brad
>-------------------------------------------------------------------
>+ Brad Daniels                  | "Oh, I know you did...          +
>+ Biles and Associates          |  but let's pretend you didn't." +
>+ These are my views, not B&A's |           - Darkman             +
>-------------------------------------------------------------------


  Run lmf instead of lmfsetup - the low-level commands are always more powerful
than the end-user interfaces...

  From lmf you can use 'list' to see which PAKs are active, then 'delete PAK_name'
to remove the ones you do not need, and finally 'register' to enter the new ones
(do no forget the 'reset' after that in order for the kernel to take these new
entries into account).

---
UNIX is a user-friendly operating system.
It just picks its friends more carefully than others.
