Some months ago, folks here bought a DECvideo/PIP board for an OSF/1 AXP,
installed it, and that was that.  I thought it might be nice to actually
use it for something, but after hunting around, I can't find any clues about
how to use the thing.  Apparently all that came with it was a very brief,
generic description of how to install the card in an AXP...no software, no
user manual, nothing.
In the DECdirect catalog, it describes this board as a "factory installed
option" and since we somehow got ahold of the board as an add-in, I'm
wondering if that has something to do with the lack of software and docs.
In the catalog, it says that the board allows video to be displayed in
a resizeable window (PIP="Picture in Picture"), and that frames can be
captured.  It also says in the fine print that no additional software is
necessary...support for DECvideo/PIP is included in OSF/1.
So I called DECdirect, and talked to people in various departments.  The
essence of those conversations is:
Q: Do we have to buy some software to make this board work?
A: No, the software is built into OSF/1.  The manual tells how to use it.
Q: Where do I get the manual?
A: If you didn't get one with the board, talk to Spare Parts.
Q: Okay, Spare Parts...what's the number for the manual?
A: The computer doesn't show one.  The board is the only component listed
   for this product...no software, no documentation.
(Additional iterations omitted.)

So, has anyone actually used DECvideo/PIP on an AXP?  Do we have the only
one in existence?  Does it have a manual?  Software?  Now there is a group
here that has the DECstation/Ultrix version of the DECvideo/PIP board, and
it comes with a software package called DECmedia, which DECdirect assures
me is not needed/does not exist for AXP OSF/1.
I will refrain from comments about the way DEC does things, since I adopted
a fatalistic view about them some years ago...
But if anyone can shed some light on this stupid board, I would be very
grateful.

