In article <2vh489$knv@usenet.srv.cis.pitt.edu>, cjk@omedsrvb.omed.pitt.edu (Carl Joel Kuzmich) writes:
>
>
>Hi all
>
>Anyone out there have any tips on what I need to place in a new kernel
>so that it will support the fast scsi or scsi-2 protocol???

OSF has supported SCSI-2, since the beginning of its time.  Fast
is just one of the SCSI-2 options.  Getting the system to negociate
with a controller to use Fast depends on a number of things.

1.  Using a controller that supports Fast transfers.  The SCSI/Fast-SCSI
    adapter for the DEC 4000 does.  The PMAZC and KZTSA TURBOchannel
    adapters do.  The KZMSA used on the DEC 7000 does as well, but getting
    short enough cables may be hard.  I don't know what the SCSI adapters
    for the DEC 2100 do.

    If you don't have controller that supports Fast transfers you're done.

2.  Use short cables.  Non-fast SCSI is limited to about 6 meters in
    cable length.  Fast SCSI is limited to 3.  Keeping the cable length
    down may be hard in some instances.  The DEC 7000 is particularly
    hard since it may use as much as a meter to get from the board to
    bulkhead.  Note: This isn't as much a problem with the KZTSA which
    also does Differential.

3.  Convince the controller to use Fast transfers.  See if the system
    console supports a command like "set fast_scsi".  The PMAZC also
    has commands to enable/disable fast SCSI on each channel.  Something
    like:
		t tc# setid [s|f] [s|f]

    The documentation that came with the controller should explain
    how to set them.

    Note: None of my DEC 3000 systems have PMAZCs, so I've never tried
    the commands to see how they really work.  I haven't tried it on
    our DEC 7000 because of the length problem.  I didn't need to
    care on the KZTSA.

4.  On OSF V1.3 and V1.3A you had to tell the kernel that it could
    negoicate Fast-SCSI with the controller.  You do this by adding
    "options	FAST_94" to the configuration and rebuilding.  V2.0
    and later don't need this.
 
