> he has no easy way to recall them to the screen.

ksh has this feature, plus a number of others.  For example, you can use 
^R<search string> followed by repeat ^R's to leap frog back through your 
history.  I could rabbit on for hours about ksh features, but instead I'll
point you at a book to buy, and attach a usefull bunch of setup commands.

The KornShell Command and Programming Language
Authors: Moris I Bolsky & David G Korn
Publisher: Prentice Hall
ISBN: 0-13-516972-0

#/ Generic ksh startup /#

# Emacs command line editing #
export VISUAL=emacs

# Settings for vi(1) #
export EXINIT='set notimeout redraw wm=5 ai aw sw=1 ic'

# Terminal chars for job control #
stty intr ^C
stty susp ^Z

# Map the arrow keys #
alias __A=^P
alias __B=^N
alias __C=^F
alias __D=^B

# Set the prompt. Show the cpu name with the domain name stripped out, # 
# the history number, and directory basename                           #
cpu=`uname -n`
PS1='"${cpu%%.*} ! ${PWD##*/}> "'

# Use a unique history file #
[[ -f ~/.$$_history ]] && rm ~/.$$_history
export HISTFILE=~/.$$_history

# Clean up history file on logout #
trap  '
      [[ -f $HISTFILE ]] && rm $HISTFILE
      ' 0


