and robustness of the new versions of the debuggers.

There is a lot of confusion lately about how dbx and DECladebug fit in
together and I thought I would offer an explanation to help
clarify the situation.  Digital offers two debuggers as part of the base
operating system: dbx and DECladebug. DECladebug is Digital's newest 
technology for debugging. It is an object oriented (C++) framework for the 
development of debugging tools. It provides an alternative to dbx for
program debugging.  The primary difference between DECladebug and
dbx is the level of language debugging; DECladebug has C++ (where 
dbx doesn't) and DECladebug has more Fortran, COBOL, and Ada support than
does dbx. (At the moment, DECladebug does not provide kernel debugging
capabilities.)  For those, users familiar with dbx command
syntax, DECladebug will feel natural. Digital is gradually migrating
to the use of DECladebug.

Speaking specifically for DECladebug (which I manage), the new release will
be including:

	- Significant language enhancements for Fortran, Fortran-90.
	- Continued leadership support for C++ (templates, exceptions,
	  multiple inheritance, and overloaded functions and operators).
	- A Motif GUI that can be used against dbx or DECladebug
	  This GUI has multiple views, a button bar, an natural/easy-to-use
	  interface and good performance. The GUI is customizable and
	  can be used either stand-alone or as a part of the FUSE environment.
	  (This GUI is replacing the current FUSE Debugger GUI.) There is also
	  a command window.
	- Support for Kernel and CMA thread debugging
	- Additional features to support Ada and COBOL debugging.
	- Source-level remote debugging support for Alpha/OSF systems.
	- Dramatic improvements in performance.
	- A significant expansion of the size of applications that can be
	  run under the debugger.
	- Attach (and detach) to a running process.
	- Real*16 support
	- Xtaso (short pointer) support for very large applications

All these features will be shipped in the operating system packages in the
upcoming OSF release.  DECladebug will be backwards compatible with the
OSF V2.0 OS release.

The dbx version that is coming out with this next OSF version is expected
to include: 

	- Real*16 support
	- Support for Kernel and CMA thread debugging
	- Attach and detach from a running process
	- Debug across fork call
	- Xtaso (short pointer) support for very large applications

BTW, this note is intended to clarify the state and plans of dbx and
DECladebug and not meant to minimize the value ofthe Totalview debugger. 
Totalview has great support fo multi-process debugging and has a nice GUI 
to boot. I think that the fact that customers have an alternative set of 
debuggers is good.

