OSF/1 is basically the union of 4.3BSD and System V (with some slight
differences), and in particular the networking is directly based on
the 4.3BSD sockets mechanism.  So you should expect things like
SO_REUSEADDR to exist, and indeed it does.

From "man setsockopt":
            SO_REUSEADDR
                Specifies that the rules used in validating addresses sup-
                plied by a bind() function should allow reuse of local
                addresses.  This option takes an int value.

Regarding "not having access to an Alpha": actually, you do.  Anyone
(at least, in the US and certain other countries) can get an account
on axposf.pa.dec.com, which is meant for "test driving" the system.

To establish an account, you should telnet or rlogin to
axposf.pa.dec.com (Internet address: 16.1.0.14). Use "axpguest" as the
account name (no password is required, just hit return), and answer the
qualifying questionnaire. After your application has been screened,
your account information will be mailed to you along with usages rules
and restrictions.

There are actually several guest-use hosts (for either OSF/1 or VMS use):

DEC OSF/1 AXP:

   axposf.pa.dec.com: A DEC 4000/710 running DEC OSF/1, located in Palo
   Alto, CA

